WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.770
Film Courage: When you were hired as a cinematographer
on your first film how prepared were you

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:10.800
that first day? Bruce Logan, cinematographer/writer/director: I think the first
live-action film I shot was

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:16.920
for a guy called Roy Cannon who was
Stanley Kubrick's prop master on 2001

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:25.619
and he had this little dark, noir 
mystery that he wanted to film so we

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:35.280
shot in them in a three-story walk-up in
London and you know I have to say

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.930
ignorant so arrogance, I don't know which
it was? You know it's like I knew how to

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.190
load an Arriflex
and I had a couple of sun lamps which a

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.879
handheld lights and I knew how to read
an exposure meter and I said I could do

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:56.730
this yeah and that was the first
live-action tour my I show as as a DP

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.879
for somebody else you know I obviously
shot stuff for myself growing up but you

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:09.750
know that was the first so I was
probably very am prepared but I think

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:19.110
they have a natural error bent for it
you know and then the next live-action

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:28.290
feature that I shot was with a friend
that he was making canopy a music movie

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:39.049
and so it was it was a very bizarre it
was my was like a long music video and I

00:01:39.049 --> 00:01:46.950
shot that and I have a knack for moving
people into lighting that was really

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:57.509
good so I I don't think I used the light
on that movie but I was able to move the

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:02.100
action of the movie into really great
lighting situations and shoot with

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.700
natural light and everybody everybody
looked at the footage and say hey this

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.330
is really great it looks so natural and
you know alive you know and that's

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:16.569
because it was you know so there wasn't
until much later that I learned about

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:23.840
lighting and then I think as I learned
about lighting I think my I think might

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:32.000
work worse in terms of it had a very
kind of lit look to it and then once I

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.799
had mastered those tools I was able to
use those same tools to create very

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:45.920
naturalistic lighting but with with
artificial sources so there was a

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.209
progression that but luckily I don't I
never screwed up bad enough not to get

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:55.250
the next gig so you know so the old
saying you need to know the rules before

00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:02.659
you can break them kind of thing applies
to the lighting yes I think so but also

00:03:02.659 --> 00:03:11.510
I think the last night alone that I tell
people that ask me what what they should

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:18.349
do is you know what should I be should I
get a job as a loader and then be an

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.359
assistant cameraman and then be an
operator and then be a DP and my answer

00:03:23.359 --> 00:03:30.040
that if you want to be a DP that you go
ahead and be a DP and start shooting and

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:36.230
you know learn learn from what you see
from your results and you're probably

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.760
going to find a level as you work your
way up that you're you're good enough

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.000
for a particular project you know but I
think taking the taking the bit by the

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:56.449
horns and saying I'm going to do this
job and just try your hardest every

00:03:56.449 --> 00:04:03.199
opportunity that you get to shoot then
that that that was my path not the or

00:04:03.199 --> 00:04:08.660
not the conventional path you know at
what point in your career and which

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:14.810
project where you headed off to set and
you realize wow this is I you know I

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.039
need to be really prepared I can't mess
up and then it might have gotten in your

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:21.430
head a little bit because the stakes
were higher

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.419
you know everybody kind of hopes to get
to a certain point and then there's a

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:29.710
new problem of Wow no there's all this
pressure on you right I think the most

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:36.820
pressure I ever felt was I did a project
called incredible shrinking woman which

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:43.000
was a movie with Lily Tomlin and I had
decided to do all the visual if I was

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.289
the effects the visual effects director
and the director of photography and I

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:53.050
decided to do all the only effects is
rear projection and front projection

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:59.080
which is a technique where you shoot the
actors in front of the life-life plates

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.660
that are being projected so once you
send that film to the lab that's you're

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:12.220
done there's no optical process there's
no post process to that footage and this

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:17.320
was I mean I was kind of out on a limb
with that so I was using equipment that

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.849
hadn't been used for a long time which
was VistaVision

00:05:20.849 --> 00:05:28.870
rear projectors and front projection
equipment and I think there was a there

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:35.590
was a time when that process wasn't
going really well you know and I felt

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:42.270
that I felt scared that the process
wasn't going to come together you know

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:52.990
but it did so it paid off you know but I
think I don't think there's ever a day

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:59.860
that I go to work if you know if I if I
wake up for a shoot day and I'm not

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:04.810
nervous or I haven't had a chip last
night the night before you know then

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:10.599
there's something wrong I'm not doing my
job you know so it's always been it's

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:15.460
always been very stressful because if
you're not stressed then I don't think

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:19.449
you do any job and then that's a great
point because I feel like now especially

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:24.370
in the kind of current state there's
like this I got this mentality and it's

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.710
almost considered a negative I feel like
to some people to show or to be nervous

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:32.960
and I'm not talking about fumbling and
shaking and

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:39.050
tripping over yourself nervous but just
to have some humanity to it and and you

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:44.990
you know it almost seems like now
there's a new breed of of mindset any

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:48.440
generation of just sort of this I got
this and yeah I'm fine

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:54.169
well I think a lot of people's work that
I see these days that are kind of up and

00:06:54.169 --> 00:07:00.669
coming there's a bunch of stuff that
they don't know that they don't know so

00:07:00.669 --> 00:07:07.820
so they've got what they think is
required but if you're going to take it

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:13.880
to a higher art form there's a lot more
than you need to know in order to get

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:18.500
there but I think it's I think it's a
combination you've got to have that you

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:22.610
gotta have that attitude that you know I
can do this it's going to be great but

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:26.510
you've also got to have you you know
you've got to know realistically what

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:30.919
your problems are going to be and to do
everything that you can to overcome

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:36.610
those so I think I think it's a
combination of the two things you know

00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:44.770
you

